    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between a certificate of analysis and a certificate of conformance?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A certificate of analysis reports the actual test results for one specific batch against its specification, signed by the quality unit that released it. A certificate of conformance states that a product or lot meets stated requirements without showing test results. The first gives evidence you can check; the second gives an assurance you must trust."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is a certificate of compliance the same as a certificate of conformance?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In most commercial use, yes. Both names describe a statement that goods meet stated requirements, usually without test results. What matters is not the name but the content: which requirement is cited, which lot is covered, and who signs. For medical devices, a declaration of conformity is a separate formal document and should not be confused with either."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a certificate of conformance replace a certificate of analysis for an API?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Not on its own. A buyer's quality unit normally decides whether to accept each batch of an API or excipient by comparing test results with a specification. A conformance statement gives it nothing to compare. Ask for the manufacturer's certificate of analysis for that batch, and treat a supplier who cannot provide one with caution."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What makes a certificate of conformance worth relying on?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Three things: it cites an exact requirement with its revision, it identifies the lot or order it covers, and it is issued under a quality management system the buyer has assessed. A conformance statement that names no requirement, or comes from a party with no visible quality system, gives very little assurance."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should both documents be requested for the same shipment?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Sometimes. A supplier may issue a certificate of analysis for the material and a certificate of conformance against the purchase order. That is acceptable when the batch numbers match and both name the same manufacturer and site. State in the purchase order or quality agreement which documents must travel with each batch."
      }
    }
  ]
